From e05d4197dc30a5b47d82ae3006f438113831520a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angel Martinez <110217098+anmartinez1256@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 22:23:48 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 45/49] Y-banded fused sweep for grids whose y-z planes pass
 the cache

The fused sweep walks each thread's x-slab in whole y-z planes. Once four
planes of voltages and currents no longer fit a thread's share of the L2
cache, the "line behind" that the sweep reuses has left the cache and
the rate falls: on a Mac Studio (M5 Max, 18 threads) the plain box went
from 7477 MC/s at 2 M cells to 3778 at 30 M.

Engine_Multithread::FusedBands cuts every block of a slab, except the
slab's last block, into bands of H y lines: per band the voltage box
hooks and UpdateVoltagesY of the block, then the currents of the lines
behind on the rows one line back in y (row 0 joins the first band, the
last band runs to NY). The slab's last block and the line before the slab
keep whole planes, so the neighbour-flag scheme between threads is as it
was. In band mode every hook runs as a box hook (extensions that count
copies, such as the steady-state detection, see one kind of call).

OPENEMS_YBAND = 0 (off) | H (force) | auto (default when unset).
auto: the share of a thread is the smallest L2 size per core of the
performance levels the threads occupy (sysctl hw.perflevelN.l2cachesize /
cpusperl2; else the shared cache over the threads; OPENEMS_YBAND_CACHE
gives MiB a thread). Bands when four planes exceed the share; H = half the
share over four lines, at least 4; off when two bands would not fit NY.
All or nothing: an extension with hooks but without box hooks keeps
whole planes. The tiled sweep, when asked for, takes precedence.

Excitation, lumped RLC and steady-state box hooks find their points by
binary search over (x, y) keys (extensions/engine_ext_boxruns.h).

Bit-identical to the unbanded sweep in every configuration tested (raw
fields and probes: H from 1 to NY-1, 1 to 6 threads, flags off, wider
blocks; at 8 M and 30 M cells at 6, 12 and 18 threads). Mac Studio,
upstream bench.py with a source, 18 threads, MC/s before -> after:
30 M ideal 3726 -> 5682, 30 M pml 2701 -> 4132, 8 M ideal 4753 -> 6045,
8 M pml 2990 -> 3670; 1 M unchanged (bands stay off).

